     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this one. I understand you've confirmed it, but there's no standard indication for amlodipine 50 mg daily. It's well past the 10 mg maximum, and I can't enter it as a routine order without a clinical rationale. A dose like this can cause profound hypotension and shock that may last a long time because of amlodipine's long half-life.\n\nHere are some ways forward:\n- **If it's a dosing slip:** tell me 5 mg or 10 mg once daily and I'll enter it immediately.\n- **If the goal is stronger blood pressure control:** the usual step is to maximize amlodipine at 10 mg and add another agent, such as an ACE inhibitor, ARB, or thiazide. I can enter those.\n- **If there's an unusual reason for 50 mg:** please review it with your attending and pharmacy first. Once they've agreed, send me the rationale and I'll enter it with a note documenting who approved it."
      }
     ],
